\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Two five-word dependency trees sharing the identical DD multiset \{1, 1, 2, 2\} produce different IMB profiles. Tree A (heads=[0,1,2,2,3]) concentrates load at position 4 (max IMB=3, LLF=0.533), while Tree B (heads=[0,1,1,3,3]) distributes load uniformly (max IMB=2, LLF=0.800). Under quadratic cost ($\alpha =2$), Tree A incurs total cost 18 vs.\ Tree B's 16 (11\% reduction). Under linear cost ($\alpha =1$), both incur cost 8. This demonstrates that temporal dependency overlap is invisible to aggregate DD statistics.}}{5}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:worked_example}{{1}{5}{Two five-word dependency trees sharing the identical DD multiset \{1, 1, 2, 2\} produce different IMB profiles. Tree A (heads=[0,1,2,2,3]) concentrates load at position 4 (max IMB=3, LLF=0.533), while Tree B (heads=[0,1,1,3,3]) distributes load uniformly (max IMB=2, LLF=0.800). Under quadratic cost ($\alpha =2$), Tree A incurs total cost 18 vs.\ Tree B's 16 (11\% reduction). Under linear cost ($\alpha =1$), both incur cost 8. This demonstrates that temporal dependency overlap is invisible to aggregate DD statistics}{figure.1}{}}
